1
00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.503
Welcome to Long-Term

2
00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:06.606
Agentic Memory with LangGraph
built in partnership with LangChain.

3
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.243
I'm delighted to welcome back Harrison
Chase, co-founder and CEO of LangChain.

4
00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.478
Thanks, Andrew.

5
00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:14.481
Recently we've seen many
agentic applications being built,

6
00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:17.984
and this has helped us to develop
a mental framework that is useful

7
00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.987
to think about
for when you're adding memory to agents.

8
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:24.891
We would like to share that with learners.
More and more AI applications

9
00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:29.229
persist over time, and this really drives
the need for agent memory.

10
00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:32.332
An example would be
an AI personal assistant.

11
00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:35.101
Exactly. Assistants are a great example.

12
00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:38.104
The more they learn, the better
they are at future tasks.

13
00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:42.409
To add memory to an agent,
you must first figure out what information

14
00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:45.478
to store in long term memory
and also when it comes

15
00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:50.478
time to use the information,
what to retrieve. First, on what to store:

16
00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:55.422
Chatbots initially just saw conversational
history in the context memory

17
00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.391
at each turn of a conversation.

18
00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:02.028
But agents that act for you
over time need long term memory.

19
00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:07.128
For example, a calendar agent might need
to persist information about meetings

20
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:11.704
over long periods and across
multiple invocations of the agent.

21
00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.340
Then comes retrieval.

22
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.545
Retrieval will take information from
memory and insert it into the context.

23
00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.649
Harrison will show you how to figure out
when and what to retrieve.

24
00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:27.687
And additionally, you also need to decide
when to update the stored information.

25
00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:28.955
So should it be updated

26
00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:33.293
through each iteration of an agent loop
or maybe in the background over time?

27
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.627
Right.

28
00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:35.795
To address these questions,

29
00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:38.798
we've found that it's useful
to think about three types of memories.

30
00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.166
Semantic Memories:

31
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:43.269
These are facts like important birthdays
for a calendar agent.

32
00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.705
The next is Episodic Memories:

33
00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:49.275
These are experiences that can help
an agent remember how to do tasks.

34
00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:53.713
And finally, there's Procedural Memories,
which are rules for an agent to follow.

35
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.617
To help manage memory,
we've created a new library, Langmem,

36
00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:01.187
that supports a vector database
that provides searchable,

37
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:05.592
shareable, persistent storage
that can be updated immediately

38
00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:08.995
by the agent
or in the background by a helper agent.

39
00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:10.430
In this course,

40
00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:14.501
you'll build a useful email assistant
that demonstrates all of these concepts

41
00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.169
using Langmem.

42
00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:18.771
Several people have worked to create this
course.

43
00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:22.675
I'd like to thank from LangChain,
Lance Martin, Will Fu-Hinthorn,

44
00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:27.213
and Nuno Campos. And from DeepLearning.AI
Geoff Ladwig.

45
00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.183
All right,
let's get started on the first lesson.

